---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="Resources">
  <h1>Resources</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The primary sources, in one place. Specs first, reference systems second, practitioner material
    last.
  </p>

  <h2>W3C specifications & guidance</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/">WCAG 2.2</a>: the accessibility Recommendation; AA is the common conformance target.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/quickref/">How to Meet WCAG (Quick Reference)</a>: filterable view of every success criterion with techniques.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/">ARIA Authoring Practices Guide</a>: keyboard and ARIA specs for every common widget.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/wai-aria-1.2/">WAI-ARIA 1.2</a>: the roles/states/properties spec itself.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/html-aria/">ARIA in HTML</a>: which roles are allowed on which elements.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/">W3C Web Accessibility Initiative</a>: hub for tutorials, evaluation, policy.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.w3.org/International/">W3C Internationalization</a>: language, direction, and text guidance.</li>
    <li><a href="https://tr.designtokens.org/format/">Design Tokens Format Module</a>: DTCG interchange format draft.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.w3.org/community/design-tokens/">Design Tokens Community Group</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Platform documentation</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web">MDN Web Docs</a>: HTML, CSS, JS, and accessibility reference.</li>
    <li><a href="https://web.dev/">web.dev</a>: performance, Core Web Vitals, and coded patterns.</li>
    <li><a href="https://semver.org/">Semantic Versioning</a>: the versioning contract most libraries follow.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Reference design systems</h2>
  <p>Public systems worth mining for structure, docs style, and decisions (not for copying wholesale):</p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://designsystem.digital.gov/">USWDS</a>: US federal; strong on accessibility rationale.</li>
    <li><a href="https://design-system.service.gov.uk/">GOV.UK Design System</a>: every pattern backed by published research.</li>
    <li><a href="https://carbondesignsystem.com/">Carbon (IBM)</a>: mature governance and contribution docs.</li>
    <li><a href="https://polaris.shopify.com/">Polaris (Shopify)</a>: content guidance done well.</li>
    <li><a href="https://m3.material.io/">Material 3 (Google)</a>: token architecture at scale.</li>
    <li><a href="https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/">Apple Human Interface Guidelines</a>: platform conventions for native surfaces.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Accessibility tooling & practice</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://webaim.org/">WebAIM</a>: contrast checker, screen-reader survey, techniques.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.deque.com/axe/">axe (Deque)</a>: automated testing engine and browser extension.</li>
    <li><a href="https://inclusive-components.design/">Inclusive Components</a>: deep worked examples of accessible widgets.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Designing for AI</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://pair.withgoogle.com/guidebook/">Google PAIR People + AI Guidebook</a>: patterns for AI-driven UX.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/haxtoolkit/">Microsoft HAX Toolkit</a>: guidelines for human-AI interaction.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Practitioner material</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://atomicdesign.bradfrost.com/">Atomic Design by Brad Frost</a>: the layered mental model, free online.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.designsystems.com/">designsystems.com</a>: guides and case studies from many teams.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.nngroup.com/articles/">Nielsen Norman Group</a>: usability research summaries.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
